1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,261
You pick up your phone.

2
00:00:02,261 --> 00:00:04,626
It looks at your face, and it opens.

3
00:00:04,626 --> 00:00:07,910
Inside, a network of numbers decided: this is you.

4
00:00:07,910 --> 00:00:10,604
But that network did not start out smart.

5
00:00:10,604 --> 00:00:13,297
Before training, its numbers were random.

6
00:00:13,297 --> 00:00:14,348
It knew nothing.

7
00:00:16,683 --> 00:00:20,060
So how does a pile of random numbers learn to see?

8
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:25,937
Today we follow one small network, from its first wrong guess to its first real answer.

9
00:00:25,937 --> 00:00:27,153
Its job is simple.

10
00:00:27,153 --> 00:00:29,585
Is this picture a cat, or not a cat?

11
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:39,807
First, what does the network actually see?

12
00:00:39,807 --> 00:00:40,535
Not a cat.

13
00:00:40,535 --> 00:00:41,482
Only numbers.

14
00:00:41,482 --> 00:00:46,359
Zoom into any photo, and it breaks into tiny squares called pixels.

15
00:00:46,359 --> 00:00:49,271
Each pixel is one number for brightness.

16
00:00:49,271 --> 00:00:50,290
Zero is black.

17
00:00:50,290 --> 00:00:51,236
One is white.

18
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:57,246
Our cat picture is sixteen pixels wide and sixteen tall.

19
00:00:57,246 --> 00:00:59,936
That is two hundred and fifty six numbers.

20
00:00:59,936 --> 00:01:03,202
To the network, this cat is just a list of numbers.

21
00:01:05,831 --> 00:01:08,674
Now meet the basic building block: a neuron.

22
00:01:08,674 --> 00:01:10,547
Think of a neuron as a judge.

23
00:01:10,547 --> 00:01:12,357
Every pixel gives it a vote.

24
00:01:12,357 --> 00:01:15,523
But the judge does not trust every vote the same.

25
00:01:15,523 --> 00:01:19,141
Some count a lot, some a little, and some count against.

26
00:01:21,447 --> 00:01:24,076
These levels of trust are called weights.

27
00:01:24,076 --> 00:01:28,309
The neuron multiplies each pixel by its weight and adds it all up.

28
00:01:28,309 --> 00:01:30,810
Then it adds one more number, the bias.

29
00:01:30,810 --> 00:01:32,285
This is a weighted sum.

30
00:01:34,344 --> 00:01:39,053
Then the sum passes through a simple function, often one called ReLU.

31
00:01:39,053 --> 00:01:41,988
If the sum is negative, the output is zero.

32
00:01:41,988 --> 00:01:44,514
If it is positive, it passes through.

33
00:01:44,514 --> 00:01:46,425
This bend is a nonlinearity.

34
00:01:46,425 --> 00:01:50,111
Without it, many layers would be no stronger than one.

35
00:01:50,111 --> 00:01:51,408
Remember this bend.

36
00:01:51,408 --> 00:01:52,910
We will need it later.

37
00:01:56,441 --> 00:01:58,707
One neuron cannot recognize a cat.

38
00:01:58,707 --> 00:01:59,706
So we use many.

39
00:01:59,706 --> 00:02:01,571
A row of neurons is a layer.

40
00:02:01,571 --> 00:02:05,436
Each neuron looks at the same pixels with its own weights.

41
00:02:05,436 --> 00:02:08,767
Their outputs become the inputs of the next layer.

42
00:02:10,975 --> 00:02:15,377
At the end, one final neuron gives a number between zero and one.

43
00:02:15,377 --> 00:02:19,034
That is the network's guess: how likely is this a cat?

44
00:02:19,034 --> 00:02:22,420
Our small network has about four thousand weights.

45
00:02:22,420 --> 00:02:25,197
Real vision networks often have millions.

46
00:02:28,294 --> 00:02:29,938
Here is the strange part.

47
00:02:29,938 --> 00:02:35,591
Before training, every weight is a small random number, like a shuffled deck of cards.

48
00:02:35,591 --> 00:02:38,287
So the network has no idea what a cat is.

49
00:02:38,287 --> 00:02:39,536
We show it our cat.

50
00:02:39,536 --> 00:02:41,771
It answers: zero point four eight.

51
00:02:41,771 --> 00:02:43,020
Almost a coin flip.

52
00:02:46,168 --> 00:02:49,379
To learn, the network must know how wrong it was.

53
00:02:49,379 --> 00:02:52,591
We measure that with one number, called the loss.

54
00:02:52,591 --> 00:02:54,820
The right answer was one, for cat.

55
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:59,015
A common choice here is the log loss, also called cross entropy.

56
00:02:59,015 --> 00:03:02,358
For this guess, it is about zero point seven three.

57
00:03:04,952 --> 00:03:08,823
A guess of zero point nine nine would give a loss near zero.

58
00:03:08,823 --> 00:03:12,372
A guess of zero point zero one would give a large loss.

59
00:03:12,372 --> 00:03:15,082
Confident wrong answers are punished most.

60
00:03:15,082 --> 00:03:18,502
Training has one goal: make the average loss smaller.

61
00:03:21,532 --> 00:03:23,967
Now think of every weight as a knob.

62
00:03:23,967 --> 00:03:26,266
Turn a knob, and the loss changes.

63
00:03:26,266 --> 00:03:30,122
With only two knobs, we can draw the loss as a landscape.

64
00:03:30,122 --> 00:03:32,151
The ground is the two weights.

65
00:03:32,151 --> 00:03:33,707
The height is the loss.

66
00:03:33,707 --> 00:03:35,466
Valleys mean good guesses.

67
00:03:37,834 --> 00:03:40,371
Our random network starts somewhere high.

68
00:03:40,371 --> 00:03:42,042
It wants to reach a valley.

69
00:03:42,042 --> 00:03:46,622
But with four thousand knobs, this landscape has four thousand dimensions.

70
00:03:46,622 --> 00:03:47,736
Nobody can see it.

71
00:03:50,401 --> 00:03:53,122
Imagine standing on a mountain in thick fog.

72
00:03:53,122 --> 00:03:54,729
You cannot see the valley.

73
00:03:54,729 --> 00:03:58,995
But you can feel the ground under your feet, and which way goes down.

74
00:03:58,995 --> 00:04:01,221
So you take one small step that way.

75
00:04:01,221 --> 00:04:03,447
Then you feel again, and step again.

76
00:04:05,727 --> 00:04:07,893
That slope is called the gradient.

77
00:04:07,893 --> 00:04:10,759
It is a list of numbers, one for each weight.

78
00:04:10,759 --> 00:04:15,600
Each one says: if this weight grows a little, how much does the loss change?

79
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,613
To go downhill, we move every weight a little the opposite way.

80
00:04:21,976 --> 00:04:25,020
The size of each step is the learning rate.

81
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:27,638
Too small, and training is very slow.

82
00:04:27,638 --> 00:04:32,876
Too large, and we jump over the valley and bounce around, or even fly off.

83
00:04:32,876 --> 00:04:36,414
Choosing it well is part science, part experience.

84
00:04:39,533 --> 00:04:41,363
But here is a big question.

85
00:04:41,363 --> 00:04:45,701
We need one slope for every weight, thousands, or even millions.

86
00:04:45,701 --> 00:04:48,887
Testing each weight one by one is far too slow.

87
00:04:48,887 --> 00:04:53,563
The answer is the most famous idea in deep learning: backpropagation.

88
00:04:55,941 --> 00:04:58,600
Think of a football team that lost a match.

89
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:01,567
The coach asks how much each player is to blame.

90
00:05:01,567 --> 00:05:04,411
The final shot depended on the pass before it.

91
00:05:04,411 --> 00:05:06,698
That pass depended on the one before.

92
00:05:06,698 --> 00:05:09,851
So blame flows backward, from the end to the start.

93
00:05:12,217 --> 00:05:14,823
Backpropagation does this with the loss.

94
00:05:14,823 --> 00:05:20,427
It starts at the output, where the error is clear, and moves back one layer at a time.

95
00:05:20,427 --> 00:05:24,532
At each step it asks: how much did this neuron affect the next?

96
00:05:24,532 --> 00:05:27,334
It multiplies these local effects together.

97
00:05:27,334 --> 00:05:29,679
In calculus, this is the chain rule.

98
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:33,905
Remember the ReLU bend?

99
00:05:33,905 --> 00:05:37,829
If a neuron's sum was negative, its output was flat at zero.

100
00:05:37,829 --> 00:05:42,407
So its slope is zero, and no blame passes through it for that picture.

101
00:05:42,407 --> 00:05:45,350
The bends decide which paths carry the blame.

102
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:48,617
And it is fast.

103
00:05:48,617 --> 00:05:54,062
One backward pass gives every slope, for roughly the cost of a few forward passes.

104
00:05:54,062 --> 00:05:58,379
The method was published in nineteen seventy by Seppo Linnainmaa.

105
00:05:58,379 --> 00:06:05,418
In nineteen eighty six, Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams showed it lets hidden layers learn useful features.

106
00:06:08,873 --> 00:06:09,925
One more trick.

107
00:06:09,925 --> 00:06:13,012
Training sets can hold millions of pictures.

108
00:06:13,012 --> 00:06:15,887
Using all of them for every step is slow.

109
00:06:15,887 --> 00:06:21,639
So we take a small random handful, maybe thirty two pictures, called a mini-batch.

110
00:06:21,639 --> 00:06:24,655
One gradient, one step, then a new handful.

111
00:06:27,208 --> 00:06:30,737
Each step is a little noisy, like a hiker who stumbles.

112
00:06:30,737 --> 00:06:33,175
But on average it still goes downhill.

113
00:06:33,175 --> 00:06:35,998
This is stochastic gradient descent, or SGD.

114
00:06:35,998 --> 00:06:39,078
Some researchers think this noise may even help.

115
00:06:39,078 --> 00:06:40,875
That is still being studied.

116
00:06:40,875 --> 00:06:42,414
We will come back to it.

117
00:06:45,610 --> 00:06:46,963
Now let's run it.

118
00:06:46,963 --> 00:06:47,997
Forward pass.

119
00:06:47,997 --> 00:06:48,395
Loss.

120
00:06:48,395 --> 00:06:49,509
Backward pass.

121
00:06:49,509 --> 00:06:49,906
Step.

122
00:06:49,906 --> 00:06:52,771
Again and again, thousands of times.

123
00:06:52,771 --> 00:06:53,964
The loss falls.

124
00:06:53,964 --> 00:06:55,794
Our cat's score climbs.

125
00:06:55,794 --> 00:06:56,987
Zero point six.

126
00:06:56,987 --> 00:06:58,340
Zero point eight.

127
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:00,090
Zero point nine seven.

128
00:07:00,090 --> 00:07:03,432
The random numbers are not random anymore.

129
00:07:06,889 --> 00:07:08,294
But what did it learn?

130
00:07:08,294 --> 00:07:09,507
We can look inside.

131
00:07:09,507 --> 00:07:14,551
In large image networks, researchers have shown what makes each neuron respond.

132
00:07:14,551 --> 00:07:17,487
Zeiler and Fergus did this in twenty thirteen.

133
00:07:17,487 --> 00:07:20,871
Olah and colleagues went further in twenty seventeen.

134
00:07:23,244 --> 00:07:24,905
A broad pattern appears.

135
00:07:24,905 --> 00:07:28,157
Early layers often respond to edges and colors.

136
00:07:28,157 --> 00:07:31,202
Middle layers respond to textures, like fur.

137
00:07:31,202 --> 00:07:34,800
Deeper layers respond to parts, like eyes and faces.

138
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,391
Nobody programmed this.

139
00:07:36,391 --> 00:07:38,467
It came from walking downhill.

140
00:07:38,467 --> 00:07:41,234
But it is a tendency, not a strict rule.

141
00:07:44,008 --> 00:07:52,437
Around nineteen sixty, Hubel and Wiesel found cells in the visual cortex of cats that respond best to edges at certain angles.

142
00:07:52,437 --> 00:07:56,518
Trained networks often learn early filters that look similar.

143
00:07:56,518 --> 00:07:59,930
So cats helped us understand how networks see cats.

144
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:02,070
Still, a network is not a brain.

145
00:08:05,552 --> 00:08:06,799
Now the honest part.

146
00:08:06,799 --> 00:08:10,168
Big networks have more weights than training pictures.

147
00:08:10,168 --> 00:08:13,162
Classical theory says they should just memorize.

148
00:08:13,162 --> 00:08:13,973
And they can.

149
00:08:13,973 --> 00:08:19,587
In twenty seventeen, Zhang and colleagues trained networks on pictures with random labels.

150
00:08:19,587 --> 00:08:21,958
The networks memorized them perfectly.

151
00:08:24,573 --> 00:08:28,739
Yet with real labels, such networks work well on new pictures.

152
00:08:28,739 --> 00:08:29,008
Why?

153
00:08:29,008 --> 00:08:34,720
One hypothesis: the noisy walk finds wide, flat valleys, which may generalize better.

154
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:38,886
But others showed that flatness depends on how you measure it.

155
00:08:38,886 --> 00:08:40,835
So it is not the whole story.

156
00:08:43,436 --> 00:08:47,828
Another idea is the lottery ticket hypothesis, from Frankle and Carbin.

157
00:08:47,828 --> 00:08:54,508
A big random network may contain small subnetworks with lucky starting weights that train well on their own.

158
00:08:54,508 --> 00:08:56,859
These are useful pieces of the puzzle.

159
00:08:56,859 --> 00:08:58,714
None is a complete answer yet.

160
00:09:01,917 --> 00:09:03,446
So, back to your phone.

161
00:09:03,446 --> 00:09:07,967
When it opens for your face, there is no little photo of you inside.

162
00:09:07,967 --> 00:09:08,831
Only numbers.

163
00:09:08,831 --> 00:09:10,160
They started random.

164
00:09:10,160 --> 00:09:14,747
Step by tiny step, they were nudged toward being a little less wrong.

165
00:09:17,005 --> 00:09:21,621
For a machine, seeing is what remains after millions of small corrections.

166
00:09:21,621 --> 00:09:24,241
Maybe learning is like that for all of us.

167
00:09:24,241 --> 00:09:25,862
Nobody starts out knowing.

168
00:09:25,862 --> 00:09:29,667
We start out wrong, and become a little less wrong each time.

169
00:09:31,908 --> 00:09:36,395
If this journey into the unseen helped you, you can subscribe to LATOON.

170
00:09:36,395 --> 00:09:38,265
There is much more to explore.

171
00:09:38,265 --> 00:09:39,386
See you next time.

